# A package installer's zip unpackers build each extracted file's path from
# the archive's own entry names: uv's streaming unzip reads
# `entry.reader().entry().filename().as_str()` out of the local header and the
# central directory (crates/uv-extract/src/stream.rs:99/:366 at the parent of
# the fixed revision), the parallel unzip reads `file.enclosed_name()` after
# `archive.by_index()` (crates/uv-extract/src/sync.rs:27), and both join the
# name onto the target directory and write through it. The names a wheel
# carries are chosen by the wheel's author, and a name another zip
# implementation would not produce -- empty, or carrying control characters --
# extracts differently here than in the scanners and installers expected to
# vet the same wheel: the archive a reviewer sees is not the archive that
# lands, so a malicious payload hides behind an improbable filename
# (CWE-20; the advisory is a zip parsing differential).
#
# The fixed revision validates the member name before it is used -- reject empty names
# and any name matching `\p{C}` -- at all three sites, in a new crate-local
# helper `validate_archive_member_name` consulted behind the existing
# `UV_INSECURE_NO_ZIP_VALIDATION` opt-out. The traversal walk (`enclosed_name`
# with its ParentDir/CurDir arms) is present in both
# revisions, and the fixed revision's own comment states the defect is not traversal.
#
# Measured, both revisions of crates/uv-extract, three `--cache off` runs
# each: 6 findings on the parent and 6 on the fixed revision, the same rule mix
# (VYQL-DOS-042 x2 on the two `unzip` contexts, VYQL-PATH-083 and
# VYQL-DOS-042 on the tar unpacker, VYQL-MEM-006 x2, VYQL-CRY-001 on md5) --
# nothing on either zip unzip, no difference between the revisions. Findings
# in the crate are not coverage of the defect.
#
# Two stacked blockers, both measured:
#
# (a) The writes are not in the IR at all. Rust lowers `let x = if c { A }
#     else { B }` through `blockTail` (internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/
#     rust.go:1091 `if_expression` -> :1232 `blockTail`), which keeps only
#     each arm's final expression; every statement before the arm's tail --
#     the nested `if directories.insert(...) { create_dir_all(path) }` and
#     the whole `match File::create_new(&path)` of the streaming unzip --
#     emits no node. The graph of the vulnerable stream.rs holds zero nodes
#     for lines 128-155 and 159-262, which is where both writes live, and
#     zero nodes for the parallel unzip's `by_index` closure body. Cases 1-2
#     are the minimal pair at the finding level: the identical
# `env::var` -> `Command::new` route fires when the spawn sits in a plain if
#     statement and dies when the same statement moves one arm deep into a
#     let-bound if expression. Case 2 flips to expect when the branch
#     statements lower; that is an engine change, not a binding.
#
# (b) No vocabulary expresses the fixed revision's discriminator. A probe binding both
#     ends the way the taint route would want -- `code.ArchiveEntryName` on
#     `filename()`/`name()`/`enclosed_name()`, `code.ArchiveEntryWrite` on
#     `create`/`create_new`/`create_dir_all` -- labels four sources in
#     stream.rs and still reports zero VYQL-PATH-004, because of (a). With
#     (a) closed the route fires on both revisions identically: the fixed revision keeps
#     the same name flowing to the same join and create, and only the new
#     helper's error return gates it. That helper is the fixing commit's own
#     crate-local name -- the rename test kills any binding keyed on it --
#     and the pre-fix stream.rs carries 25 `validate`-named tokens
#     (`insecure_no_validate`, `skip_validation` x24) to the fixed revision's own
#     `insecure_no_validate` moved to lib.rs: a "validate"-named marker
#     suppresses the vulnerable streaming unzip outright and credits the
#     parallel one for an env-var opt-out reader, wrong in both directions.
#     std has no character-validation vocabulary to key a neutraliser on
#     (`is_empty` is ordinary Rust everywhere), and the traversal vocabulary
#     the existing path-join siblings key on (`ParentDir`, `CurDir`,
#     `components`) is present in both revisions because the traversal walk
#     is never the defect.
#
# Reach: 8 .rs files in the whole uv tree carry the by_index /
# next_with_entry / enclosed_name shape, and 0 files across the ~86 other
# corpus clones -- a binding family for archive member names would fire on
# this tree alone, a concept per case.
#
# Cases 3-5 model the real unpackers (the streaming local-header site, the
# parallel by_index site, and the patched form) and pin the miss: all three
# stay reject today, and 3-4 flip to expect only when (a) closes AND an
# archive-entry-name source binding exists in Rust; case 5 stays reject
# because the patched form carries the same flow through the same join, so
# without a neutraliser vocabulary the route cannot separate the revisions at
# all.

test "rank1900: environment command spawn inside a plain if statement -- the route control"
  lang rust
  file src/main.rs
  code
  ```
  use std::process::Command;

  pub fn run_stmt(flag: bool) -> Result<(), std::io::Error> {
      let cmd = std::env::var("SH_CMD").map_err(|e| std::io::Error::other(e))?;
      if flag {
          let child = Command::new(&cmd).spawn()?;
          let _ = child;
      }
      Ok(())
  }

  fn main() {
      let _ = run_stmt(true);
  }
  ```
  expect VYQL-INJ-002

